            <fr:title text="The batch size is the intensity">The batch size is the intensity</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>Card</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="anki">1786316439311</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="anki-deck">MLSys</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
  <html:p>For a bf16 matmul <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[[B, D] \times  [D, F] \to  [B, F]]]></fr:tex>, give the FLOPs, bytes, intensity, and the resulting rule of thumb.</html:p>
  
  
  
 <html:div class="jms-callout" style="
  margin: 1.1rem 0;
  padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 0.9rem;
  border-left: 2px solid rgb(63, 83, 127);
  "><html:span style="font-weight: 500; color: rgb(63, 83, 127);" /> 
  <html:details><html:summary><html:span class="foldout" style="font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 11pt; font-weight: 500; color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.65);">Answer</html:span></html:summary>
  <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\text {FLOPs} = 2BDF]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\text {bytes} = 2BD + 2DF + 2BF]]></fr:tex> — both operands loaded, the output written, two bytes apiece — so

  <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[\mathrm {Intensity} = \frac {BDF}{BD + DF + BF}]]></fr:tex>

  With <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B \ll  D, F]]></fr:tex> the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[DF]]></fr:tex> term dominates the denominator and the intensity collapses to <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[BDF / DF = B]]></fr:tex>. The whole rule of thumb is that <html:em>the batch size is the intensity</html:em>: a matmul is compute-bound once the per-replica batch exceeds <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[240]]></fr:tex> tokens on TPU (<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\approx  295]]></fr:tex> on H100, from <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[9.89 \times  10^{14} / 3.35 \times  10^{12}]]></fr:tex>).

  Three caveats. <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B]]></fr:tex> counts <html:em>tokens</html:em>, not sequences. The threshold is per replica — sharding scales FLOPs and bandwidth together, so it applies to each copy of the weights (<fr:link href="/QPFO/" title="Data parallelism" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/QPFO/" display-uri="QPFO" type="local">Data parallelism</fr:link>). And tiling changes the byte count once the matmul is large enough that the operands no longer fit.
</html:details>
</html:div>



